   "notes": "Unanswered. No pool page discloses or credibly reports revenue for Grounding with Bing, Web IQ, or Bing's API or syndication business. Microsoft reports only its Search advertising segment: $15.176B in FY2026 and $13.878B in FY2025, covering Bing, Copilot, News, Edge and third-party affiliates. Its filings mention partnerships through which it provides and monetizes search offerings, without amounts. The only other figure is a 2023 New Street estimate for Bing's ad revenue. Useful new context: WIRED said the Bing APIs still had thousands of customers at retirement. PPC Land says Yahoo's Scout runs on the Bing grounding API. Web IQ is listed on Microsoft Marketplace as a first-party consumption service with no price. From FY2027 Microsoft folds LinkedIn advertising into the line (AdScaleLab), which makes Bing even harder to isolate. Errors: c1 is false. The cited Web IQ page names Nasdaq Boardvantage in a testimonial, contradicting 'no customers by name'. c16 is unsupported: the terms page it cites has no $14 price. c49 is unverifiable: windowsnews.ai redirects to windowsforum.com, which returned 403. c29 (Web IQ's data sources) doesn't bear on revenue. SEC.gov returned 403, so I read the FY2026 and FY2025 10-Ks from Wayback and R45.htm (c10) through WebFetch. Secondary pages with figures that contradict the filings, and weren't credited: PPC Land (r25, r36) says FY2025 search revenue grew 21% (10-K: 13%, ex-TAC 20%). Skillademia (r3) claims $18B of 2022 ad revenue. FourWeekMBA (r2) puts the segment at ~11% of Microsoft revenue. cloro (r27) still quotes $35 per 1,000 against the current $14. searchengineland (r4) and backlinko (r26) returned 403. PitchBook (r31) is about an unrelated company named Web-IQ."
